eviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Check the **reporting clause, opening quotation mark, capital and closing punctuation**.","A comma introduces the full quoted sentence, which begins with a capital and ends with punctuation.","Choose **She said, “Prepare the evening meal.”**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The opening quotation mark is missing.","B":"A comma introduces the full quoted sentence, which begins with a capital and ends with punctuation.","C":"This version lacks the capital for the full quoted sentence and final punctuation.","D":"The comma wrongly separates the subject and verb, and the full quotation starts lowercase."}}},{"id":"3edad3ba-1e6c-57bd-a9a4-0eb13b4f1921","topicSlug":"punctuation-clauses","level":"B1","prompt":"“____ time to prepare the evening meal.” Choose the contraction meaning “it is”.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"I’ts"},{"id":"B","text":"It’s"},{"id":"C","text":"Its"},{"id":"D","text":"Its’"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Expand the word: the sentence needs **it is**.","explanation":"**It’s** contracts **it is** in this sentence.","keywords":["its-contraction"],"skill":"its-contraction","contextKey":"punctuation-clauses-49","fingerprint":"877f7f4cafde64f4071caa0a7e1a1332cbe834ba82bc6418b72c74250a66e1d7","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Expand the word: the sentence needs **it is**.","**It’s** contracts **it is** in this sentence.","Choose **It’s**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The apostrophe is misplaced; it replaces the missing letter in is.","B":"**It’s** contracts **it is** in this sentence.","C":"Its is a possessive determiner, not the requested contraction.","D":"Its’ is not the standard contraction or possessive form."}}},{"id":"dcf866aa-3a3c-5924-a6d5-aed8cdc14d97","topicSlug":"punctuation-clauses","level":"B1","prompt":"Choose punctuation that joins two main clauses without a conjunction: “We repaired the rowing boat ____ the supervisor thanked us.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"no punctuation"},{"id":"B","text":";"},{"id":"C","text":","},{"id":"D","text":"—,"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Both sides can stand as sentences; use a **semicolon** when joining them without a conjunction.","explanation":"A **semicolon** can connect these related independent clauses.","keywords":["semicolon-main-clauses"],"skill":"semicolon-main-clauses","contextKey":"punctuation-clauses-50","fingerprint":"4ef57afc4be9bbcdfe5b3c4b0291a55db65de337b175c1ff7699487e2d01571c","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Both sides can stand as sentences; use a **semicolon** when joining them without a conjunction.","A **semicolon** can connect these related independent clauses.","Choose **;**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The two independent clauses would run together.","B":"A **semicolon** can connect these related independent clauses.","C":"A comma alone would create a comma splice in formal prose.","D":"A dash-comma combination is not the standard boundary required here."}}},{"id":"1346968d-625c-586e-a69f-50046afb207a","topicSlug":"punctuation-clauses","level":"B1","prompt":"Where should the comma go in “After we repaired the rowing boat we rested”?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"After, we repaired the rowing boat we rested."},{"id":"B","text":"After we, repaired the rowing boat we rested."},{"id":"C","text":"After we repaired, the rowing boat we rested."},{"id":"D","text":"After we repaired the rowing boat, we rested."}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Find where the **introductory after-clause ends**.","explanation":"The comma separates the introductory after-clause from the main clause we rested.","keywords":["introductory-clause-comma"],"skill":"introductory-clause-comma","contextKey":"punctuation-clauses-50","fingerprint":"b0df587178c632bf2cb79a6267d7d6fad2668930e8000bee9e4278ccd2215242","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Find where the **introductory after-clause ends**.","The comma separates the introductory after-clause from the main clause we rested.","Choose **After we repaired the rowing boat, we rested.**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This separates the subordinating word from its clause instead of marking the clause boundary.","B":"This separates the subject from its verb.","C":"This separates the transitive verb from its object.","D":"The comma separates the introductory after-clause from the main clause we rested."}}},{"id":"664b7d11-97b0-5c78-a24b-e6bb56776082","topicSlug":"punctuation-clauses","level":"B1","prompt":"Choose a correctly introduced direct instruction to repair the rowing boat.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"She, said “repair the rowing boat.”"},{"id":"B","text":"She said, repair the rowing boat”."},{"id":"C","text":"She said, “Repair the rowing boat.”"},{"id":"D","text":"She said “repair the rowing boat”"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Check the **reporting clause, opening quotation mark, capital and closing punctuation**.","explanation":"A comma introduces the full quoted sentence, which begins with a capital and ends with punctuation.","keywords":["direct-speech-capital"],"skill":"direct-speech-capital","contextKey":"punctuation-clauses-50","fingerprint":"b97b7c2f8f3d0d571d7e3659af643aea9755cc720067bccc99cba37b82f60121","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Check the **reporting clause, opening quotation mark, capital and closing punctuation**.","A comma introduces the full quoted sentence, which begins with a capital and ends with punctuation.","Choose **She said, “Repair the rowing boat.”**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The comma wrongly separates the subject and verb, and the full quotation starts lowercase.","B":"The opening quotation mark is missing.","C":"A comma introduces the full quoted sentence, which begins with a capital and ends with punctuation.","D":"This version lacks the capital for the full quoted sentence and final punctuation."}}},{"id":"05b15c78-e712-599a-a89d-1c907946153b","topicSlug":"punctuation-clauses","level":"B1","prompt":"“____ time to repair the rowing boat.” Choose the contraction meaning “it is”.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"I’ts"},{"id":"B","text":"It’s"},{"id":"C","text":"Its"},{"id":"D","text":"Its’"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Expand the word: the sentence needs **it is**.","explanation":"**It’s** contracts **it is** in this sentence.","keywords":["its-contraction"],"skill":"its-contraction","contextKey":"punctuation-clauses-50","fingerprint":"c8751f54555cf1f955e50a60a5c005d3f25812f7574728951d79be64733266d5","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Expand the word: the sentence needs **it is**.","**It’s** contracts **it is** in this sentence.","Choose **It’s**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The apostrophe is misplaced; it replaces the missing letter in is.","B":"**It’s** contracts **it is** in this sentence.","C":"Its is a possessive determiner, not the requested contraction.","D":"Its’ is not the standard contraction or possessive form."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Use punctuation to show clause relationships, possession, quotation and sentence boundaries.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Independent clauses need a real boundary","explanation":"A comma alone does not normally join two independent clauses in formal prose. Use a full stop, a semicolon, or a comma with a suitable coordinating conjunction. A semicolon joins closely related main clauses without a conjunction.","pattern":"The train stopped**;** we got off.","commonMistakes":["The train stopped, we got off is a comma splice in ordinary formal writing."],"examples":["Choose punctuation that joins two main clauses without a conjunction: “We prepared the visitor guide **;** the supervisor thanked us.”","Choose punctuation that joins two main clauses without a conjunction: “We restored the old portrait **;** the supervisor thanked us.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Colons and introductory material","explanation":"A colon can introduce an explanation or list after a complete clause. A comma commonly follows an introductory subordinate clause or a long introductory adverbial. A dash can mark a break or inserted explanation, with conventions varying by style.","pattern":"We need three things**:** paper, ink and time.","commonMistakes":["Do not routinely put a colon between a verb and its object: We need: paper is usually unnecessary punctuation."],"examples":["Where should the comma go in “After we prepared the visitor guide we rested”? → **After we prepared the visitor guide, we rested.**","Where should the comma go in “After we restored the old portrait we rested”? → **After we restored the old portrait, we rested.**"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Direct speech","explanation":"Quotation marks show exact spoken or written words. A reporting clause can introduce the quotation with a comma. Capitalise the beginning of a full quoted sentence. Single versus double quotation marks and some punctuation placement vary by house style.","pattern":"She said, **“Please wait.”**","commonMistakes":["Reported speech normally removes quotation marks because it does not claim the same exact wording."],"examples":["Choose a correctly introduced direct instruction to prepare the visitor guide. → **She said, “Prepare the visitor guide.”**","Choose a correctly introduced direct instruction to restore the old portrait. → **She said, “Restore the old portrait.”**"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Apostrophes and capitals","explanation":"Use apostrophes for contractions and possession, not ordinary plurals. Its is possessive; it’s means it is or it has. Capitalise sentence openings, the pronoun I and proper names, including languages and weekdays.","pattern":"**It’s** Tuesday. / The dog lost **its** collar.","commonMistakes":["Quotation style varies, but the distinction between its and it’s is grammatical rather than an optional style choice."],"examples":["“**It’s** time to prepare the visitor guide.” Choose the contraction meaning “it is”.","“**It’s** time to restore the old portrait.” Choose the contraction meaning “it is”."],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["The train stopped**;** we got off.","We need three things**:** paper, ink and time.","She said, **“Please wait.”**","**It’s** Tuesday. / The dog lost **its** collar."]},"stage":"5 · Complex sentences","prerequisites":["noun-clauses"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
